  <id>n8n_sync</id>
  <name>n8n Sync</name>
  <summary>Your n8n workflows as native, editable, backed-up Nextcloud files</summary>
  <description><![CDATA[
n8n Sync surfaces your n8n workflows as native Nextcloud files. Map one or more n8n
tags to folders and every tagged workflow shows up as a `.n8n.json` file — with a
real file icon, deep links back to n8n, and a per-folder sync mode.

- **Sync** — full two-way ownership. Edit a workflow in the Files app, over WebDAV,
  or from your desktop client and the change pushes back to n8n; a sync folder
  doubles as a complete, restorable backup of every workflow in it.
- **Link** — a lightweight pointer (id, name, URL) that opens the workflow in n8n.

Sync is reconcile-based: re-running a pull never duplicates files, because the link
between a file and its workflow is a stable id stored in the file's metadata — not
the filename — so renaming, moving, and restoring never break the connection.

This is a community integration and is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or sponsored
by n8n GmbH. "n8n" and the n8n logo are trademarks of n8n GmbH, used here only to
identify the service this app integrates with.
  ]]></description>
  <version>0.1.5</version>
  <licence>agpl</licence>
  <author>Kelly Ferrone</author>
  <namespace>N8nSync</namespace>
  <category>integration</category>
  <category>files</category>
  <!--
    DECLARED SO THE APP IS LOADED ON WEBDAV REQUESTS. This is the missing half of
    the trash-purge bug (saga §5.7), and it is invisible until you look for it.

    `register()` runs for every enabled app during bootstrap, but `boot()` only runs
    when an app is actually LOADED — and remote.php, the WebDAV entry point, loads a
    restricted set:

        $appManager->loadApps(['authentication']);
        $appManager->loadApps(['extended_authentication']);
        $appManager->loadApps(['filesystem', 'logging']);

    With no <types>, this app was never in that set, so boot() never ran on a DAV
    request. Everything wired in register() — the copy, move, rename, create and
    soft-delete listeners — kept working, which is exactly what made the gap so hard
    to see: only the ONE thing wired in boot(), the legacy trash-purge hook, was
    silently dead. A perfectly correct hook, connected in a method that never ran.

    `filesystem` is the type for apps that must be present when the filesystem is in
    play, which is what a file-event-driven mirror is.
  -->
  <types>
    <filesystem/>
  </types>
